    "slot_key": "Role-based access control for regulated records",
    "slot_value": "Access should be role-based so the master, crew, owner, shore office, and possibly a compliance user each see only what they need for their job; users should be able to view and amend only the records they are responsible for, sensitive commercial or personal details should be protected from general access, and an audit trail should record who changed what and when. In practice, permissions should follow the person’s current operational role rather than staying fixed forever, so temporary cover may require temporary access; if someone leaves the vessel or company, access should be removed promptly; any temporary permissions should expire automatically or be revoked once the cover ends; and amending records outside normal responsibility should be restricted to cases with a clear reason and traceable approval, with the exact business rule still to be confirmed. The detailed audit history should be restricted, with crew seeing only their own vessel records and authorised owners, masters, and enforcement or administration users having access as appropriate. Detailed audit history entries for records viewed, exported, or changed by support on behalf of a vessel should be visible only to people with a genuine need to know, typically the vessel’s authorised master and owner, and relevant fisheries administration or enforcement users; support staff can see the history for cases they handled but not records outside their assigned scope. The vessel involved matters, and access through a shared or delegated device should be visible in the history, but it does not by itself grant extra viewing rights.",
