[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-5-2-dyn-11",
    "slot_key": "Quick entry assistance and error-reduction controls",
    "slot_value": "Support quick selection lists, sensible defaults for non-declaration fields, and a clear review step before submission so crews can enter data quickly without increasing mistakes. The system should also force extra confirmation or validation before submission when something looks unusual, such as a quantity far outside the normal range, a species choice that does not fit the area or gear used, or a port or vessel detail that conflicts with the voyage context. Extra checks are also needed when required fields are missing, when values were imported from another onboard system, or when the record is submitted after a long interruption. When required transhipment details are missing, the crew should see a clear validation message explaining what is missing and why it matters, and the interface should take them directly to the field they need to fix.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-5-2-dyn-10",
    "slot_key": "Exceptional event handling for immediate logbook completion",
    "slot_value": "If interrupted, the system should automatically save a draft and allow resumption later without losing entered data, provided it is still clear the record has not yet been submitted. Drafts should stay editable for as long as they’re still part of the active voyage, but once the voyage or relevant activity has ended, they should either be submitted, explicitly abandoned, or reopened only through a controlled correction process. Poor weather, device failure, or a temporary outage should pause the pressure to complete the draft, but only while the same voyage context is still valid. An automatic timeout that wipes work is considered risky offshore and should be avoided. If it’s just a temporary interruption, the draft should remain open and effectively paused so the crew can pick up where they left off without re-entering anything. If the interruption becomes long enough that the voyage context changes, or the system has reason to think the draft may be stale, it should require a review of the saved data before continuing. Once access is regained, the crew should resume the same draft rather than start over unless the underlying trip or activity has clearly moved on. If a required transhipment detail is genuinely unavailable at sea, the crew should be allowed to mark that reason and keep the report in a pending or corrected state only if the rules permit it. If the omission is due to an external source problem or a crew mistake, they should correct it immediately or use the approved fallback if the source is down, then sync it later.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-4-3-2",
    "slot_key": "Error handling for missing, inconsistent, or out-of-range values",
    "slot_value": "If data is missing, duplicated, or clearly out of range, the system should warn the user and either hold the record for correction or allow submission only with an explanation; the exact validation rules still need confirmation. For transhipment reports specifically, the default should be to block submission and require the crew to complete any missing required detail first, because those fields are part of the compliant report. A correction flag should be allowed only in tightly limited cases where the missing item is genuinely unavailable at sea but the crew can prove it will be corrected through the proper follow-up process. The rule should change only for exceptions agreed with the fisheries administration, or where the system can clearly show the omission came from an approved external source and not from the crew skipping the field. When a required transhipment detail is missing, the system should show a clear validation message saying what is missing and why it matters, and take the user straight to the field they need to fix. If the omission is due to genuine at-sea unavailability, the crew should be allowed to mark that reason and keep the report in a pending or corrected state only if the rules allow it. If it is an external source problem or a crew mistake, they should correct it immediately or use the approved fallback and sync it later if the source is down.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

